Q: Why my code in finding the max. depth of a tree fails? A: <blockquote>
<p>Why my code in finding the max. depth of a tree fails?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>OP is using an invalid algorithm.<br />
The <code>while</code> loops look like an incorrect attempt for a speedy solution.</p>
<p>Instead, step back to basics.</p>
<p>Alternate algorithm:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// Pseudo code
int maxDepth(struct TreeNode* root) {
  if root is NULL, return 0;
  left_side_depth = maxDepth(the left side);
  right_side_depth = maxDepth(the right side);
  return 1 + the greater of the 2 sides.
}
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>Now consider how code could do fewer recursions.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p>Sample realization of fewer recursions.  Only recurse when <code>.left</code> and <code>.right</code> are non-<code>NULL</code></p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>int maxDepth(const struct TreeNode *node) {
  int depth = 0;
  while (node) {
    while (node-&gt;left) {
      depth++;
      if (node-&gt;right) {
        int left = maxDepth(node-&gt;left);
        int right = maxDepth(node-&gt;right);
        return depth + (left &gt; right ? left : right);
      }
      node = node-&gt;left;
    }

    if (node-&gt;right) {
      depth++;
      node = node-&gt;right;
    }
  }
  return depth;
}
</code></pre>
<p>To avoid much recursion, the below is untested code.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>size_t maxDepth2(const struct TreeNode *node) {
  size_t depth_recursive = 0;
  size_t depth = 0;
  while (node) {

    while (node-&gt;left) {
      depth++;
      if (node-&gt;right) {
        int right = depth + maxDepth2(node-&gt;right);
        if (right &gt; depth_recursive) {
          depth_recursive = right;
        }
      }
      node = node-&gt;left;
    }

    if (node-&gt;right) {
      depth++;
      node = node-&gt;right;
    }
  }
  return depth &gt; depth_recursive ? depth : depth_recursive;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Inherit wrapper from abstract method A: <p>You can achieve this<a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/65383999/13629335"> using <code>super</code> to delegate</a> from your <em>child</em> <code>Extend</code> up to your <code>Base</code>.<br />
It's important that you use the decorators in the right order, so <code>abc.abstractmethod</code> still works.</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>import abc

def test(method):
    def wrapper(self, *args):
        # your wrapper functionality here
        return func(self, *args)
    return wrapper


class Base(abc.ABC):

    @abc.abstractmethod
    @test
    def some_method(self):
        ...
</code></pre>
<p>And then you implement it like this. It will throw an error when it isn't defined and the wrapper works as intended.</p>
<pre><code>class SomeClass(Base):

    def some_method(self, *args):
        super().some_method(*args)
</code></pre>
